WEBVTT

00:00.255 --> 00:05.547
At one in the afternoon, Service Analytics' report shows 100%: the percentage of

00:05.547 --> 00:07.782
completed deliveries that arrived on time.

00:07.782 --> 00:09.934
Unfinished parcels are excluded.

00:09.934 --> 00:12.012
This is the page's invented incident.

00:12.012 --> 00:15.252
Does the report's refresh time tell us when the number was calculated?

00:15.252 --> 00:16.426
Pause and explain.

00:21.425 --> 00:22.041
No.

00:22.041 --> 00:24.404
This report reads a saved result.

00:24.404 --> 00:26.718
Refreshing it does not rerun the job.

00:26.718 --> 00:31.041
To explain this number, follow the matching identifiers, starting with the refresh

00:31.041 --> 00:34.232
record, rather than simply inspecting the latest successful run.

00:35.281 --> 00:39.973
Refresh F1300 read summary-R1205.

00:39.973 --> 00:42.778
Follow that exact output identifier.

00:42.778 --> 00:47.466
Its matching output was published at twelve oh five, fifty-five minutes before the

00:47.466 --> 00:50.962
refresh, and names producing run R1205.

00:50.962 --> 00:53.089
We now have the execution to investigate.

00:54.083 --> 00:59.658
The run points to saved input S1205 and retained code completed-v1.

00:59.658 --> 01:03.606
A snapshot is a saved copy of the input at a particular time.

01:03.606 --> 01:07.012
Would today's deliveries table be enough to reproduce this result?

01:07.012 --> 01:08.621
Explain before continuing.

01:13.737 --> 01:14.662
No.

01:14.662 --> 01:16.798
Today's data and code may differ.

01:16.798 --> 01:19.665
Inspect this run's retained input and calculation.

01:19.665 --> 01:24.134
At twelve oh five, two parcels had arrived on time and two were unfinished.

01:24.134 --> 01:26.047
All four were due by noon.

01:26.047 --> 01:28.040
Assume the invented records are complete.

01:29.063 --> 01:32.427
The retained calculation excludes the unfinished parcels.

01:32.427 --> 01:35.975
Two completed deliveries remain, and both were on time.

01:35.975 --> 01:38.411
Two out of two gives 100%.

01:38.411 --> 01:42.072
The stored input and executed code explain this particular result.

01:42.917 --> 01:46.057
This is how matching records establish provenance for a number.

01:46.057 --> 01:50.566
Lineage describes the data's route and dependencies; provenance records a particular

01:50.566 --> 01:52.454
dataset or result's history.

01:52.454 --> 01:53.629
The terms overlap.

01:53.629 --> 01:56.993
Explaining why the result was 100% does not establish

01:56.993 --> 01:59.575
whether it was still current at the report refresh.

01:59.575 --> 02:01.187
Chapter 4 examines that.

02:02.178 --> 02:04.667
Try the page's metadata-map exercise.

02:04.667 --> 02:08.954
Start at Report refresh, choose Example record, and

02:08.954 --> 02:11.728
follow its output to Published output, then Run.

02:11.728 --> 02:14.231
Find the retained input and code version.

02:14.231 --> 02:19.250
Explain why the displayed result was 100%, then use Check your answer on the page.
